<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Generic airship, SITL development model - SHARED BASE BODY.
  ============================================================
  Hull, gondola, tail and sensors only; NO propulsion. Spawn the
  variant wrapper instead - it merge-includes this base and adds the
  vectored side propulsion:
    airship - vectored side propulsion (rod through the CoM)
  Comments stay study-guide verbose until upstreaming.

  GEOMETRY (origin at gondola bottom center = ground contact point)
    hull:    VISUAL ellipsoid, semi-axes 5.25 x 1.25 x 1.25 m
             (10.5 m long, 2.5 m diameter), center at z = 2.05.
             COLLISION box 9.8 x 2 x 2 at the same center - see the
             mass budget for why the buoyancy shape is a box.
    gondola: box 1.25 x 0.8 x 0.8 m, center at z = 0.40
    fins:    two thin crossed plates at the stern, VISUAL ONLY
             (no collision: negligible volume, and aerodynamics will come
             from plugins in a later increment, not from contacts)

  MASS BUDGET (buoyancy-neutral EXACTLY, by design)
    The buoyancy plugin integrates the COLLISION volumes. An ellipsoid
    volume contains pi, so no finite-decimal mass can ever match it
    (the residue was a measured ~0.4 g excess = 1.3 mm/s climb). Box
    collisions have rational volumes, and V = 40 (no factor of 3) makes
    every DERIVED quantity finite-decimal too - CoM, CoV, arm, inertias:
      displaced volume V = 9.8*2*2 + 1.25*0.8*0.8
                         = 39.2 + 0.8 = 40.000 m^3
      lift = 1.225 kg/m^3 * 40 m^3 = 49.000 kg   (1.225 = 49/40)
      hull (envelope + fins):  12.000 kg at z = 2.05
      gondola (all systems):   36.000 kg at z = 0.40
      pods + propellers:      2 x (0.4 + 0.1) kg (added by the wrapper)
      total                    49.000 kg = lift -> neutral, no ballast
    Mass-split rule: gondola divisible by 12 and hull by 5 keep the box
    and ellipsoid inertia formulas finite-decimal as well.

  PROPULSION
    None here. The wrapper adds two tilt pods + propellers (their
    2 x 0.5 kg is reserved in the budget above) on the transverse rod
    through the CoM at z = 0.8125.

  THE PENDULUM (every value below is exact, none rounded)
    center of mass   CoM = (12*2.05 + 36*0.40) / 48 = 39/48
                         = 0.8125 m (pods/props sit at the CoM plane,
                           so they do not move it)
    center of volume CoV = (39.2*2.05 + 0.8*0.40) / 40 = 80.68/40
                         = 2.017 m
    metacentric arm = CoV - CoM = 1.2045 m
    Buoyancy pulls UP at the CoV, weight DOWN at the CoM: tilt the ship
    and the pair forms a righting couple ~ m*g*arm*sin(angle)
      = 49 * 9.8 * 1.2045 = 578.4009 N*m per radian (small angles).
    Undamped roll oscillation, hand model: T = 2*pi*sqrt(Ixx/578.4)
      = 2*pi*sqrt(38.1/578.4) ~ 1.6 s. MEASURED in simulation: ~2.5 s;
    discrepancy unresolved (suspect the hand model's inertia reference
    vs the engine's); righting direction, restoring magnitude scale and
    neutrality are the verified parts.

  VERIFY (standalone, env sourced, GZ_IP=127.0.0.1)
    spawn, teleport to altitude: it holds (neutrality is now exact by
    construction - hands-off drift is solver noise, not net lift).
    tilt it:  gz service -s /world/lta/set_pose ... roll ~0.35 rad
      -> it swings back and oscillates around upright.
    gz model -m airship - -link   (mind the space) shows base mass 48.0
    and the inertia below; View -> Center of Mass shows the marker low,
    inside the hull's lower half - the pendulum, visible.
-->
<sdf version="1.10">
  <model name="airship_base">
    <self_collide>false</self_collide>
    <static>false</static>
    <link name="base_link">
      <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <!-- Combined CoM of hull + gondola (pods/props carry their own
             2x0.5 kg in their own links AT the CoM plane; total stays
             49.000 kg = neutral). Low CoM + high CoV = pendulum. -->
        <pose>0 0 0.8125 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>48.0</mass>
        <!-- Composite inertia about the CoM (parallel-axis by hand).
             The hull keeps the SOLID-ELLIPSOID inertia model (the
             physical envelope), even though its collision is a box -
             collisions feed buoyancy/contacts, not inertia:
             hull (solid ellipsoid): Ixx = m(b^2+c^2)/5           =  7.5
                                     Iyy = Izz = m(a^2+b^2)/5     = 69.9
               + hull offset dz = 2.05-0.8125 = 1.2375: m*dz^2    = 18.376875 (to Ixx, Iyy)
             gondola (box):          Ixx = m(y^2+z^2)/12          =  3.84
                                     Iyy = Izz = m(x^2+z^2)/12    =  6.6075
               + gondola offset dz = -0.4125: m*dz^2              =  6.125625 (to Ixx, Iyy)
             Totals: Ixx = 35.8425, Iyy = 101.01, Izz = 76.5075
             (all finite-decimal, exact - see the mass-split rule) -->
        <inertia>
          <ixx>35.8425</ixx>
          <iyy>101.01</iyy>
          <izz>76.5075</izz>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
        </inertia>
        <!-- HULL ADDED MASS: excluded (see the MODEL SCOPE note near the
             end of this file). It was prototyped as SDF <fluid_added_mass>
             (Li & Nahon 2007 prolate-spheroid values, about the CV:
             diag(3.45, 36.2, 36.2, 0, 134.9, 134.9) kg / kg m^2), but
             measurement showed the engine applies it to wrenches on this
             link while forces from the propeller child links - the only
             excitation path used here - get the plain rigid response.
             A plant inconsistent between force paths is worse than a
             rigid one, so it stays out. -->
      </inertial>
      <!-- HULL collision: the buoyancy volume, as a BOX (39.2 m^3 exactly;
           rational on purpose - see the mass budget). Also the shape that
           matters if the ship ever rolls fully over; the box stays inside
           the visual footprint's length and slightly proud of its beam. -->
      <collision name="hull_collision">
        <pose>0 0 2.05 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>9.8 2 2</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
      </collision>
      <!-- GONDOLA collision: the ground-contact shape (and 0.8 m^3 of
           buoyancy volume, included in the mass budget). -->
      <collision name="gondola_collision">
        <pose>0 0 0.4 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>1.25 0.8 0.8</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <surface>
          <contact>
            <ode>
              <min_depth>0.001</min_depth>
              <max_vel>0</max_vel>
            </ode>
          </contact>
          <friction>
            <ode />
          </friction>
        </surface>
      </collision>
      <visual name="hull_visual">
        <pose>0 0 2.05 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <ellipsoid>
            <radii>5.25 1.25 1.25</radii>
          </ellipsoid>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.85 0.85 0.9 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.85 0.85 0.9 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.3 0.3 0.3 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="gondola_visual">
        <pose>0 0 0.4 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>1.25 0.8 0.8</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.3 0.3 0.35 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.3 0.3 0.35 1</diffuse>
          <specular>0.2 0.2 0.2 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <!-- Cruciform tail as two crossed plates, VISUAL ONLY (deliberate
           inverse of the collision/visual lesson: geometry physics cannot
           feel). Centered at x = -4.3, within the hull's tapering stern. -->
      <visual name="fin_horizontal_visual">
        <pose>-4.3 0 2.05 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>1.0 3.6 0.04</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.6 0.2 0.2 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.6 0.2 0.2 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <visual name="fin_vertical_visual">
        <pose>-4.3 0 2.05 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>1.0 0.04 3.6</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <ambient>0.6 0.2 0.2 1</ambient>
          <diffuse>0.6 0.2 0.2 1</diffuse>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <!-- ================== SENSORS ==================
           Unchanged from the cube increment; all sit at the link origin,
           i.e. the bottom of the gondola - where avionics live on a real
           airship. Chain: gz sensor -> gz_bridge -> uORB -> EKF2. -->
      <sensor name="air_pressure_sensor" type="air_pressure">
        <gz_frame_id>base_link</gz_frame_id>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>50</update_rate>
        <air_pressure>
          <!-- Noise modeled after BMP390 -->
          <pressure>
            <noise type="gaussian">
              <mean>0</mean>
              <stddev>3</stddev>
            </noise>
          </pressure>
        </air_pressure>
      </sensor>
      <sensor name="magnetometer_sensor" type="magnetometer">
        <gz_frame_id>base_link</gz_frame_id>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>100</update_rate>
        <magnetometer>
          <!-- Same values as x500. Upstream caveats apply here too:
               noise stddev is interpreted in tesla while the sensor
               reports gauss (gz-sim units quirk, see gz-sim PR 2460);
               intent per upstream: ~3 mgauss RMS, IIS2MDC-class. -->
          <x>
            <noise type="gaussian">
              <stddev>0.0001</stddev>
            </noise>
          </x>
          <y>
            <noise type="gaussian">
              <stddev>0.0001</stddev>
            </noise>
          </y>
          <z>
            <noise type="gaussian">
              <stddev>0.0001</stddev>
            </noise>
          </z>
        </magnetometer>
      </sensor>
      <sensor name="imu_sensor" type="imu">
        <gz_frame_id>base_link</gz_frame_id>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>250</update_rate>
        <imu>
          <angular_velocity>
            <!-- Noise modeled after IIM42653, 0.05 deg/s in rad/s -->
            <x>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>0.0008726646</stddev>
              </noise>
            </x>
            <y>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>0.0008726646</stddev>
              </noise>
            </y>
            <z>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>0.0008726646</stddev>
              </noise>
            </z>
          </angular_velocity>
          <linear_acceleration>
            <!-- Noise modeled after IIM42653, 0.65/0.70 mg-rms in m/s^2 -->
            <x>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>0.00637</stddev>
              </noise>
            </x>
            <y>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>0.00637</stddev>
              </noise>
            </y>
            <z>
              <noise type="gaussian">
                <mean>0.0</mean>
                <stddev>0.00686</stddev>
              </noise>
            </z>
          </linear_acceleration>
        </imu>
      </sensor>
      <sensor name="navsat_sensor" type="navsat">
        <gz_frame_id>base_link</gz_frame_id>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>30</update_rate>
      </sensor>
    </link>
    <!-- ============ MODEL SCOPE (indoor free-flight validation) ============
         The simulated plant is exactly: rigid body + buoyancy + the two
         vectored thrusters added by the wrapper. Deliberately excluded, as
         second-order for low-speed initial-response tests in still air:
           - hull ADDED MASS (also blocked by an engine limitation;
             note inside <inertial> above)
           - hull DRAG. Coefficients were validated earlier (terminal
             6.5 m/s at 16 N) and are kept for later outdoor scenarios;
             re-enable via a gz-sim-hydrodynamics-system plugin with:
             <xUabsU>-0.301</xUabsU> <yVabsV>-10.1</yVabsV>
             <zWabsW>-10.1</zWabsW> <kPabsP>-0.5</kPabsP>
             <mQabsQ>-80.0</mQabsQ> <nRabsR>-80.0</nRabsR>
             <xU>-0.5</xU> <yV>-2.0</yV> <zW>-2.0</zW>
             <kP>-15.0</kP> <mQ>-40.0</mQ> <nR>-40.0</nR>
           - Munk moment, fin/gondola added mass, near-wall effects
           - fin aerodynamics: no effect in still air; fins are
             visual-only geometry -->
    <!-- FRAME MAPPING to the validation datum (CV-origin, FRD axes):
         this model's origin is the gondola bottom in gz world axes
         (x forward, y LEFT, z UP). The center of volume CV sits at
         (0, 0, 2.017) in model coordinates - an EXACT datum (moved from
         2.014 across the exact-buoyancy redesigns; sync DOC-253 rows on
         the next doc pass). Convert model -> CV-FRD by:
           x_frd = x_gz,  y_frd = -y_gz,  z_frd = -(z_gz - 2.017)
         (FRD: +x forward, +y starboard, +z down.) -->
  </model>
</sdf>
